          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Richard Feynman</strong><br>\nRichard Phillips Feynman was born on May 11, 1918, in Queens, New York. His parents were not scientists—they were regular people, with his father a uniform salesman and his mother staying at home. Despite this, Feynman showed an early interest in science and mechanics. By the age of ten, he had taught himself trigonometry, advanced algebra, and calculus, showing the remarkable intelligence that would define his career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Work</strong><br>\nFeynman attended the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT) for undergraduate studies and later Princeton University for his graduate studies. His doctoral thesis introduced the path integral formulation of quantum mechanics, laying the groundwork for his later work. During World War II, he was recruited to work at Los Alamos on the Manhattan Project, which developed the atomic bomb.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Manhattan Project</strong><br>\nThe Manhattan Project was a crucial period in Feynman's life. He worked with some of the greatest minds in physics, including Robert Oppenheimer. Feynman was known for his brilliant work and also for his eccentricities, such as his safe-cracking skills. Despite the intense work, he often found time to play pranks and indulge his curious nature.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
