          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Quantum Electrodynamics</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Feynman turned to academia, where he made significant contributions to the field of quantum electrodynamics (QED). His work helped solve the problem of infinities in calculations, which plagued physicists at the time. For this work, he introduced the concept of Feynman diagrams, which became a standard tool for visualizing particle interactions in QED and beyond.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nFeynman's work on QED earned him the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1965, which he shared with Julian Schwinger and Sin-Itiro Tomonaga. The trio's independent development of QED marked one of the greatest achievements in 20th-century physics. Feynman's distinctive approach and methods provided deep insights into the interaction of light and matter.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Teaching Philosophy</strong><br>\nFeynman was also a celebrated teacher, known for his ability to explain complex subjects in an accessible way. He taught at Cornell University and later at the California Institute of Technology. His lectures were compiled into the famous \"Feynman Lectures on Physics,\" which continue to be a valuable resource for students and educators.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
