          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Challenger Disaster Investigation</strong><br>\nIn 1986, Feynman played a pivotal role in the investigation of the Space Shuttle Challenger disaster. Using simple, yet critical experiments, he demonstrated how the O-rings, crucial components of the shuttle, failed in cold temperatures. His straightforward approach and refusal to be swayed by NASA's official explanations highlighted his commitment to scientific integrity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Interests</strong><br>\nFeynman was known for his lively personality and wide range of interests outside of physics, including playing the bongo drums, safecracking, and painting. He had a deep appreciation for the arts and was an enthusiastic participant in cultural activities, showcasing his belief in a well-rounded life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nFeynman passed away on February 15, 1988. He left behind a legacy as one of the most influential and charismatic scientists of his time. His contributions to physics are still felt today, and his books and lectures have inspired generations of scientists and laypeople alike. Feynman remains a symbol of intellectual curiosity and the joy of discovery.</p>\n"
              },
